    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d792">

                <pb facs="0817" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/154</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Kirby Lumber Company to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Houston, Texas</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-05">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 5, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Telegraph companies refuse to handle code messages for Porto Rico advising French
                    cable New York to Porto Rico regulations demand plain language and full address.
                    Must these revisions be complied with on messages from one part of United States
                    to another? We, of course, considering Porto Rico United States territory and
                    business in a sense interstate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirby Lumber Company</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
